## Photography — four permitted image types
1. **Texture macro** — formula close-up (serum drop, cream, gel swatch). Directional natural light, no visible background.
2. **Lab objects** — beakers, pipettes, vials on linen / sage tile / aged paper. Never 3D rendered.
3. **The label** — extreme close-up of a real INCI ingredient list (printed paper, ink, grain). The label is the truth document; this is the most on-brand “evidence” image. (Replaces botanical archive.)
4. **Skin / in-use** — hands applying product, real skin texture, tight crop. Swatches on real skin only, never white card.

Always: natural directional light, textured surfaces (linen, sage tile, aged paper, stone).
Never: ring light, white studio bg, 3D lab renders, before/after clinical comparisons, influencer selfies, cluttered flatlays.
Until real photos exist, use striped placeholders with a monospace note naming the required shot. Botanical illustration is NOT a core type — only a rare exception for genuine ingredient-origin stories, and only in scientific herbarium-plate style (specimen + Latin binomial), never floral/romantic.

## Carousel architecture (Instagram, 10 slides, 4:5 portrait 1080×1350)
1. **Cover** — product hero image + title (Bold) + category label.
2. **The verdict** — 2–3 lines, direct opinion, no hedging.
3–7. **Analysis** — one ingredient/claim per slide; percentage if known; mechanism in plain language.
8. **Swatch** — texture on skin, **no text overlay**.
9. **Comparison** — vs. category alternatives.
10. **Final call** — Lab Score: **Save / Skip / Invest**.

### The Lab Score, defined
Scored on three axes — **efficacy** (actives work at a dose that matters), **evidence** (research supports the claim), **price-to-active** (worth the cost). The verdict is set BEFORE a brand is contacted; gifting never moves it. Every final-call slide carries the method line: *scored on formulation, evidence & price-per-active.*
- **Skip (no):** formulation doesn't deliver — actives below threshold, poor delivery/stability, sabotaging irritants/fragrance, or an unsupported claim. Not worth your money at any price.
- **Save (yes, but):** a good formulation with one condition — *price* (a rival has better price-to-active; buy on a drop), *conditional* (works for a specific skin type/goal, not universal), *redundant* (doesn't beat what you likely already own), or *unproven* (promising but evidence/maturity isn't there yet). Merit is real; the purchase is qualified.
- **Invest (yes):** excellent and justifies its price — proven merit, broad benefit, correct price-to-active. Buy and recommend without asterisks.

Background rotation: Bone → Ink → Lab Mist → Petal → Bone → Ink … On Ink slides, text is Sage or Frost. Never two Ink slides adjacent. Educational carousels reuse the same architecture without a product to sell.

## Feed rhythm (Instagram grid, 6-post cycle, read left→right)
```
[COVER]  [TEXTURE MACRO]  [DATA SLIDE]
[SKIN]   [BOTANICAL]      [LAB OBJECT]
```
- Cover posts: Lab Sage bg + Ink text.
- Data slides: Ink bg + Lab Sage text.
- Texture / botanical / lab: Bone bg.
- Skin in-use: Petal bg.
- **Never two Ink posts adjacent.**

## TikTok (9:16 vertical)
- Open 0–2s: texture macro, no voiceover.
- Text overlays: DM Sans **Bold** in Bone (`#F5F0E8`) on **Ink strips**. Never native TikTok fonts.
- End card: the **seal** (molecular stamp) or LAB wordmark centred on Frost (`#ECF0EE`), static, 1.5s.

## Voice
Short sentences. One idea. Full stop. Active voice, no filler. Ingredient percentages when discussing formulas. Disclose gifted/PR in the first line. Precision over enthusiasm — we state, we do not exclaim. The silence (what we choose not to say) is part of the voice. Reference tone: *The Gentlewoman* meets *Lab Muffin Beauty Science*.
- **Archetype:** an **editor** — not a dermatologist, not an influencer. We read before we recommend; compare before we conclude; translate complexity without removing nuance; give the full answer, not the easy one.
- **Core attributes:** (1) **Intelligent** — know more than we say, demonstrate not perform. (2) **Clear** — make the reader smarter, not ourselves sound smart. (3) **Opinionated** — evidence first, then a verdict; willing to choose. (4) **Curious** — investigate claims, follow ingredients, question reputations. (5) **Elegant** — no drama, outrage, clickbait, or superiority.
- **Words we own:** formulation (not “product”), mechanism, efficacy, ratio, consider, evidence, texture, discern, formulate, *the label* (the INCI list as the truth document).
- **Words we never use:** glow, game-changer, hydrating (as superlative), natural, toxic, clean, obsessed, luxury (self-described), miracle, amazing, holy grail — and never exclamation marks.

## What this brand is NOT
Not Drunk Elephant (color maximalism), not Glow Recipe (candy-pink), not generic wellness (warm beige, cursive, crystals), not clinical/pharmaceutical (cold white, no texture), not influencer beauty (ring light, selfie, haul).

**The ship test:** does it read as a publication, or as an advertisement? We are always the former.
